Z||000000||William||01-22-2000||11:03 PM||||Got a bad one this time. When the car is moving and I really stomp on it while running through the gears, I'm getting a scraping, dragging sound thats hard to describe. It sounds sort of like a screwdriver being held against one of the pulleys while it's spinning, but it's not a steady tone. Kinda like the screwdriver being allowed to skip and hop while being held against the pulley. That make since? The sound appears to come from directly behind the center console but you know how that goes, could be comming from anywhere in the engine bay I guess. It does not make this sound while parked and reving the engine. How do you find a moving only type of sound? I pray it's not the clutch as it seems to work fine. I'm not sure what I'm looking for when staring at the engine. I see no signs of the pulleys rubbing. could it be the a/c compressor pulley? should i cut the belt and see. Any thoughts or previous problems that sound simular?  TIA<P>||||reg||9
Z||000001||batboy||01-22-2000||11:26 PM||||Sounds like a hard one to figure out. I'd start by checking the dog bone and motor mounts to make sure the engine's not shifting around on you.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Luperman||01-22-2000||11:39 PM||||Had a sound like that one about a year ago and come to find out it was the wear sensor tab on my disc brake pads (rear ones) drove me crazy !!!!!||||reg||6
Z||000003||2FAST4U||01-22-2000||11:51 PM||||Just fix a problem that was making a sound just like you described.<BR>The rear transmission mount bracket, not the mount but the bracket that bolts to the cradle was broken. It let the trans drop down just enough to let the CV joint mak contact with the cradle at times.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||01-23-2000||03:24 AM||||I'd check for a loose belt.  (No.  This isn't one of my usual stupid, smart-a** answers.)  Honestly.  My wife had the same noise on her old Camaro and it was just a belt.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||01-23-2000||06:27 AM||||Try putting the car in gear or park brake and engine off then rock the car back and forth to see if anythings moves.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wolfhound||01-23-2000||07:48 AM||||Check to see if the waterpump pully is making contact with the battery support. I had this happen with a bad motor mount on a 4 cylinder. the skipping sound was the pully bolts hitting. Look for a wear on the bolts and support.<BR>Coy <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||William||01-23-2000||08:34 AM||||I'll check some of those things as soon as it warms up alittle more. It does seem as tho it's related to the engine shifting position as it does seem to be worse when I really tork the engine hard. Only happens at higher rpm before I shift. Thanks for not mentioning the clutch. I think you guys are just trying to be nice and don't want to screw up my weekend :-)||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||01-23-2000||01:42 PM||||Definatly check all the mounts and dog bone. ||||reg||1
